<section class="case-sec" id="stakes">
  <div>
    <h2>What attribution is, and why it breaks quietly</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Finance owns the totals. They know, to the dollar, what the platform
        earned. What they don't own is <em>who influenced it</em> — which partner
        drove which account to which workload. That's attribution, and it's the
        number that decides partner investment, co-sell incentives, and which
        relationships leadership defends in a planning cycle.
      </p>
      <p>
        The failure mode that matters here isn't a crash. A crashed pipeline
        pages someone. This pipeline kept running, kept writing rows, and kept
        producing a revenue picture that looked entirely plausible — while an
        upstream source had silently stopped delivering the signal that made
        attribution possible. <strong>Plausible is the dangerous state.</strong> Nobody
        disputes a number that looks about right.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  </section>

<section class="case-sec" id="trace">
  <div>
    <h2>The trace: 20+ components, no map</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The pipeline had been built up over years by people who had since moved
        on: <strong>20+ components</strong> of ingestion, joins, enrichment, and
        roll-up, with the business logic distributed across all of them rather
        than living anywhere you could read it. There was no lineage view. The
        only way to find where the signal died was to walk it.
      </p>
      <p>
        That walk is the unglamorous core of this case file. Component by
        component: what does this stage assume about its input, what does it
        emit, and at which hop does the population of attributable accounts
        drop? The answer was an upstream source that had changed underneath
        us — still delivering data, no longer delivering <em>this</em> data.
      </p>
      <p class="aside">the lesson I took from this is the whole reason the
        <a href="/case/data-trust/" class="g">data-trust platform</a> exists.
        a pipeline that cannot tell you it's wrong is a pipeline you are
        eventually going to be wrong with.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-sec" id="recovery">
  <div>
    <h2>The recovery: an alternate feed, and six months nobody wanted</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Finding the break doesn't restore the number. The original signal wasn't
        coming back, so recovery meant <strong>sourcing an alternate telemetry
        feed</strong> that could reconstruct the same attribution facts, proving it
        agreed with history where history was still trustworthy, and then
        replaying it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then the part that doesn't fit on a résumé line: <strong>six months of
        backfills, driven through quarter close.</strong> Every restated month is a
        month where someone's reported number changes, which means every batch
        needed a reconciliation story before it landed, not after. The work ran
        as <strong>7 incidents</strong> — including a <strong>severity-2 I led across 4
        teams</strong>, because by then the blast radius crossed data engineering,
        finance reporting, and two partner-facing surfaces.
      </p>
      <p>
        Net effect: <strong>~$45M/month of partner-attributed revenue recovered</strong>
        and, more to the point, <em>sustained</em> — a recovered number that
        re-breaks in two quarters is a rounding error with extra steps.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-sec" id="redesign">
  <div>
    <h2>The redesign: last-writer-wins was the real bug</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The data loss was the trigger. The design flaw underneath it was
        <strong>last-writer-wins</strong>: when several partners touched an account, the
        pipeline credited whichever record arrived last. That's not an
        approximation of reality, it's an arbitrary function of arrival order —
        and it silently discarded most of the partner relationships the business
        was trying to measure.
      </p>
      <p>
        Rebuilt end-to-end as <strong>multi-partner credit</strong>: an account can carry
        several attributable relationships, each with its own basis, rather than
        one winner. Coverage moved accordingly — <strong>~80–90% more partner
        accounts tracked</strong> — and partner-attributed influenced revenue lifted
        <strong>&gt;10% month-over-month</strong>, not because anything about the business
        changed, but because the measurement stopped throwing away truth.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="table-panel">
    <div class="cp-head"><span>what changed in the attribution model</span><span class="g">shape only · identifiers removed</span></div>
    <div class="table-scroll">
