</section>

<section class="case-sec decision-sec" id="decision">
  <h2>The decision: trust is a number</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Revenue data is only useful if Finance can't break it — and if the pipeline can tell you when it's wrong. Rebuilding first and backfilling later would have been faster to ship and impossible to trust: a better model of worse data. So the order was recovery, then redesign, then reconciliation built in as a feature rather than run as a report.</p>
    <p>What it cost: six months of backfills through quarter close that nobody wanted to own. What it produced: ~$45M/mo recovered and sustained, &lt;1% variance against Finance, and ~80–90% more partner accounts tracked. Ask me about the six months of backfills nobody wanted to own.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-sec" id="guardrails">
  <div>
    <h2>Reconciliation as a feature, not a report</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The claim I'll defend in an interview is this one: <strong>Finance is not a
        source to copy, it's an oracle to disagree with.</strong> The pipeline
        attributes revenue independently, then reconciles its own total against
        Finance actuals — and holds <strong>&lt;1% variance</strong>. Because the two are
        derived separately, agreement is evidence rather than tautology. When
        they diverge, that's a signal with a real cause, and it fires before a
        leadership deck is built on it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Validation gates with automated backfills</strong> made the recovery
        self-service instead of heroic: when a gate fails, the affected window is
        re-derived and re-reconciled without a person driving it through a
        quarter close.
      </p>
      <p>
        The same governed foundation collapsed the <strong>monthly business
        review</strong> from a multi-day manual assembly into a
        <strong>~5-minute pipeline</strong> (<strong>&gt;95% less effort</strong>) that reconciles
        automatically and is now consumed directly by leadership — the reporting
        layer stopped being a person's week.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-sec" id="ask">
  <div>
    <h2>Questions to ask me</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        <strong>Why not rebuild first and backfill later?</strong> Because the restated
        history is what earns the right to the new design. Shipping multi-partner
        credit on top of a broken corpus would have produced a better model of
        worse data, and nobody downstream would have been able to tell which
        change moved their number.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Why six months and not a weekend?</strong> Quarter close. Restating a
        closed month is a governance event, not a job run — the constraint was
        never compute.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>What's still weak?</strong> The pipeline's ancestry is still 20+
        components. The gates and reconciliation mean it now fails loudly, but a
        rewrite onto a smaller surface is the honest next move, and it hasn't
        happened yet.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
